## The classic surprise

```jsx
function Counter() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  function handleClick() {
    setCount(count + 1);
    console.log(count);   // still 0 — not 1
  }

  return <button onClick={handleClick}>{count}</button>;
}
```

`count` is a `const` inside this render. Nothing can reassign it. `setCount` tells React "next time you render this component, use 1".

---

## State as a snapshot

Each render captures its own values:

```jsx
function handleClick() {
  setCount(count + 1);   // count is 0 → schedules 1
  setCount(count + 1);   // count is STILL 0 → schedules 1 again
  setCount(count + 1);   // → 1
}
// Final result: 1, not 3
```

---

## The functional updater

When the next value depends on the previous one, pass a function. React queues them and applies each to the result of the last:

```jsx
function handleClick() {
  setCount((c) => c + 1);   // 0 → 1
  setCount((c) => c + 1);   // 1 → 2
  setCount((c) => c + 1);   // 2 → 3
}
// Final result: 3 ✅
```

This also matters inside async code and intervals, where the captured `count` would be stale:

```jsx
// ❌ Captures count from the render that created the interval — always 0 + 1
useEffect(() => {
  const id = setInterval(() => setCount(count + 1), 1000);
  return () => clearInterval(id);
}, []);

// ✅ Reads the latest value at call time
useEffect(() => {
  const id = setInterval(() => setCount((c) => c + 1), 1000);
  return () => clearInterval(id);
}, []);
```

---

## Lazy initialisation

```jsx
// ❌ Runs on every render; the result is discarded after the first
const [data, setData] = useState(expensiveParse(raw));

// ✅ Runs once — React calls the function only on mount
const [data, setData] = useState(() => expensiveParse(raw));
```

---

## Bailing out

React compares with `Object.is`. Setting the identical value skips the re-render:

```jsx
setCount(0);           // already 0 → no re-render
setUser(sameObject);   // same reference → no re-render
```

Which is exactly why mutating state does nothing — the reference never changed.

## What batching does

```jsx
function handleClick() {
  setCount(c => c + 1);
  setFlag(f => !f);
  setName('Ada');
}
// One re-render, not three
```

Without batching you would get three renders and two intermediate frames where the UI is inconsistent.

---

## What changed in React 18

```jsx
// React 17: TWO renders — batching did not apply inside promises
fetch('/api').then(() => {
  setLoading(false);
  setData(result);
});

// React 18: ONE render
```

The same fix applies to `setTimeout`, native event listeners and any other async context. This is one of the reasons upgrading to 18 made some apps measurably faster with no code changes.

---

## Opting out

Sometimes you genuinely need the DOM updated before the next line runs — usually to measure it:

```jsx
import { flushSync } from 'react-dom';

function addRow() {
  flushSync(() => {
    setRows([...rows, newRow]);   // committed to the DOM before flushSync returns
  });
  listRef.current.scrollTop = listRef.current.scrollHeight;  // now the row exists
}
```

Without `flushSync`, the scroll happens before the new row is in the DOM and lands in the wrong place.

> `flushSync` forces a synchronous render and blocks the browser. Use it for measurement and scroll positioning, not as a general tool.

---

## Batching does not change the snapshot rule

```jsx
function handleClick() {
  setCount(count + 1);   // count is 0 → 1
  setCount(count + 1);   // count is still 0 → 1
}
// Result: 1. Batching groups the renders; it does not make `count` update mid-handler.
```

Use the functional form when order matters. Batching and staleness are separate issues that get confused constantly.

---

## Where this shows up in interviews

> "I call setState twice in a click handler. How many renders?"

One — in every React version, because handlers were always batched. The interesting follow-up is the same question inside a `.then()`, where the answer is one in React 18 and two before it.

## Why mutation silently fails

```jsx
const [user, setUser] = useState({ name: 'Ada', age: 36 });

// ❌ Nothing happens on screen
user.age = 37;
setUser(user);        // Object.is(user, user) === true → React bails out

// ✅ New object, new reference
setUser({ ...user, age: 37 });
```

The mutation *did* change the data. React just has no way to know, because it compares references, not contents.

---

## Arrays: which methods are safe

| Operation | ❌ Mutates | ✅ Returns new |
|-----------|-----------|----------------|
| Add | `push`, `unshift` | `[...arr, item]`, `[item, ...arr]` |
| Remove | `pop`, `shift`, `splice` | `filter` |
| Replace | `arr[i] = x` | `map` |
| Sort | `sort` | `[...arr].sort()` or `toSorted()` |
| Reverse | `reverse` | `[...arr].reverse()` or `toReversed()` |

```jsx
// Add
setTodos([...todos, newTodo]);

// Remove
setTodos(todos.filter((t) => t.id !== id));

// Update one item
setTodos(todos.map((t) => (t.id === id ? { ...t, done: !t.done } : t)));

// Insert at an index
setTodos([...todos.slice(0, i), newTodo, ...todos.slice(i)]);
```

---

## Nested updates get ugly fast

```jsx
// Spread is shallow — every level on the path needs copying
setState({
  ...state,
  user: {
    ...state.user,
    address: {
      ...state.user.address,
      city: 'Berlin',
    },
  },
});
```

This is the point at which people reach for **Immer**, which lets you write the mutation and produces an immutable update:

```jsx
import { produce } from 'immer';

setState(produce((draft) => {
  draft.user.address.city = 'Berlin';   // looks like mutation, is not
}));
```

Redux Toolkit has Immer built in, which is why reducers there can assign directly to `state`.

---

## Flatten instead

Deep nesting is usually a data-shape problem. Normalising it removes the spread pyramid entirely:

```jsx
// Instead of a deeply nested tree
{ usersById: { 1: {…}, 2: {…} }, orderIds: [1, 2] }
```

Updating one user is now a single-level spread.

## The signal

```jsx
// ❌ Four setters that must always move together
function submit() {
  setLoading(true);
  setError(null);
  setData(null);
  setSubmitCount(c => c + 1);
}
```

Four independent variables means invalid combinations are representable — `loading: true` with `data` already set, for instance. A reducer makes the transition atomic.

---

## The reducer version

```jsx
const initialState = { status: 'idle', data: null, error: null };

function reducer(state, action) {
  switch (action.type) {
    case 'submit':  return { status: 'loading', data: null, error: null };
    case 'success': return { status: 'success', data: action.data, error: null };
    case 'failure': return { status: 'error', data: null, error: action.error };
    default:        throw new Error(`Unknown action: ${action.type}`);
  }
}

function Form() {
  const [state, dispatch] = useReducer(reducer, initialState);

  async function handleSubmit() {
    dispatch({ type: 'submit' });
    try {
      const data = await save();
      dispatch({ type: 'success', data });
    } catch (error) {
      dispatch({ type: 'failure', error });
    }
  }

  if (state.status === 'loading') return <Spinner />;
  // …
}
```

Invalid states are now unreachable, and the transitions live in one testable function.

---

## Choosing

| Use `useState` | Use `useReducer` |
|----------------|------------------|
| One independent value | Several values that change together |
| Simple set-and-forget | Next state derived from previous in non-trivial ways |
| Two or three fields | A state machine with defined transitions |
| Logic lives in one handler | The same update logic appears in many handlers |

---

## Two under-appreciated benefits

**`dispatch` is referentially stable.** Unlike an inline callback, it never changes identity, so passing it to a memoised child or listing it in an effect's dependencies causes no churn — no `useCallback` needed.

**The reducer is a pure function.** You can unit-test every transition without rendering anything:

```jsx
expect(reducer({ status: 'idle' }, { type: 'submit' }))
  .toEqual({ status: 'loading', data: null, error: null });
```

---

## Do not overdo it

A reducer for a single boolean is ceremony. Start with `useState` and convert when the handlers start setting three things at once — that is the actual trigger.

## The anti-pattern

```jsx
// ❌ Two sources of truth
function Cart({ items }) {
  const [total, setTotal] = useState(0);

  useEffect(() => {
    setTotal(items.reduce((sum, i) => sum + i.price, 0));
  }, [items]);

  return <p>Total: {total}</p>;
}
```

Problems: an extra render on every change, `total` is briefly wrong on first paint, and any code path that forgets to update it silently breaks.

```jsx
// ✅ One source of truth
function Cart({ items }) {
  const total = items.reduce((sum, i) => sum + i.price, 0);
  return <p>Total: {total}</p>;
}
```

It cannot be out of date, because it is recomputed from the truth every render.

---

## The same mistake with filtering

```jsx
// ❌ State that mirrors other state
const [query, setQuery] = useState('');
const [filtered, setFiltered] = useState(items);
useEffect(() => {
  setFiltered(items.filter((i) => i.name.includes(query)));
}, [query, items]);

// ✅ Derive it
const [query, setQuery] = useState('');
const filtered = items.filter((i) => i.name.includes(query));
```

---

## When to memoise

Only when the calculation is genuinely expensive — sorting tens of thousands of rows, not filtering fifty:

```jsx
const sorted = useMemo(
  () => hugeList.sort(comparator),
  [hugeList, comparator]
);
```

Measure first. `useMemo` is not free, and for cheap operations the bookkeeping costs more than the calculation.

---

## The legitimate exceptions

**Adjusting state when a prop changes** is occasionally right, and there is a supported pattern for it — set state during render rather than in an effect:

```jsx
function List({ items }) {
  const [selection, setSelection] = useState(null);
  const [prevItems, setPrevItems] = useState(items);

  if (items !== prevItems) {      // during render, not in an effect
    setPrevItems(items);
    setSelection(null);           // React restarts the render immediately
  }
  // …
}
```

Usually the `key` prop is simpler:

```jsx
<List key={listId} items={items} />   // new list → fresh state, no code
```

---

## The check to run

> Could this value be wrong while the other state is right?

If yes, it should not be state.

## The classic case

```jsx
function Timer() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  useEffect(() => {
    const id = setInterval(() => {
      setCount(count + 1);   // ❌ `count` is 0 forever
    }, 1000);
    return () => clearInterval(id);
  }, []);                    // effect runs once → closure captured once

  return <p>{count}</p>;     // shows 1 and stops
}
```

The callback was created during the first render, where `count` is `0`. It computes `0 + 1` every second, forever.

---

## Fix 1 — functional updater (usually best)

```jsx
useEffect(() => {
  const id = setInterval(() => {
    setCount((c) => c + 1);   // ✅ React supplies the current value
  }, 1000);
  return () => clearInterval(id);
}, []);
```

The closure no longer needs to read `count` at all, so it cannot be stale.

---

## Fix 2 — declare the dependency

```jsx
useEffect(() => {
  const id = setInterval(() => setCount(count + 1), 1000);
  return () => clearInterval(id);
}, [count]);   // ✅ correct, but tears down and recreates the interval every second
```

Correct but wasteful here. It is the right fix when the effect genuinely needs to restart on change — a subscription to a changing channel, for example.

---

## Fix 3 — a ref for "the latest value"

When a callback needs fresh values but must not be recreated:

```jsx
function useLatest(value) {
  const ref = useRef(value);
  useEffect(() => { ref.current = value; });
  return ref;
}

function Chat({ onMessage }) {
  const latestOnMessage = useLatest(onMessage);

  useEffect(() => {
    const socket = connect();
    socket.on('message', (m) => latestOnMessage.current(m));  // always current
    return () => socket.close();
  }, []);   // socket is created once, regardless of how often onMessage changes
}
```

This is the pattern the `useEffectEvent` hook is designed to replace once it ships.

---

## How to catch these

Turn on `eslint-plugin-react-hooks` and **do not silence `exhaustive-deps`**. Nearly every stale closure bug starts with someone deleting a dependency to stop an effect re-running. If a dependency is causing problems, the effect is structured wrong — removing the warning just hides it.

## Small form — one object, one handler

```jsx
function SignupForm() {
  const [values, setValues] = useState({ email: '', password: '' });

  function handleChange(e) {
    const { name, value, type, checked } = e.target;
    setValues((v) => ({ ...v, [name]: type === 'checkbox' ? checked : value }));
  }

  return (
    <form onSubmit={handleSubmit}>
      <input name="email" value={values.email} onChange={handleChange} />
      <input name="password" type="password" value={values.password} onChange={handleChange} />
    </form>
  );
}
```

The computed key `[name]` is what lets one handler serve every field.

---

## Why this stops scaling

Every keystroke sets state, so the **entire form re-renders**. With 40 fields and validation running on each change, typing starts to lag.

---

## React Hook Form

```jsx
import { useForm } from 'react-hook-form';
import { zodResolver } from '@hookform/resolvers/zod';
import { z } from 'zod';

const schema = z.object({
  email: z.string().email('Enter a valid email'),
  password: z.string().min(8, 'At least 8 characters'),
});

function SignupForm() {
  const { register, handleSubmit, formState: { errors, isSubmitting } } =
    useForm({ resolver: zodResolver(schema) });

  return (
    <form onSubmit={handleSubmit(onSubmit)}>
      <input {...register('email')} />
      {errors.email && <span role="alert">{errors.email.message}</span>}

      <input type="password" {...register('password')} />
      {errors.password && <span role="alert">{errors.password.message}</span>}

      <button disabled={isSubmitting}>Sign up</button>
    </form>
  );
}
```

Inputs stay uncontrolled and subscribe individually, so typing in one field does not re-render the others. The Zod schema gives you validation and the TypeScript type from one definition.

---

## React 19 form actions

```jsx
import { useActionState } from 'react';

function Signup() {
  const [state, formAction, isPending] = useActionState(async (prev, formData) => {
    const result = await signUp(formData.get('email'));
    return result.error ? { error: result.error } : { success: true };
  }, null);

  return (
    <form action={formAction}>
      <input name="email" />
      <button disabled={isPending}>Sign up</button>
      {state?.error && <p role="alert">{state.error}</p>}
    </form>
  );
}
```

No `onSubmit`, no `preventDefault`, no manual loading state — and with a framework it works before JavaScript loads.

---

## Accessibility, briefly

Every input needs a real `<label htmlFor>`. Errors need `role="alert"` and `aria-invalid` so screen readers announce them. This gets skipped constantly and is a genuinely good thing to mention.

## Debounce vs throttle

| | Fires | Use for |
|---|-------|---------|
| **Debounce** | Once, after activity stops | Search input, autosave, resize-complete |
| **Throttle** | At most once per interval | Scroll position, mouse move, infinite scroll |

---

## Debouncing a value (the clean way)

```jsx
function useDebounced(value, delay = 300) {
  const [debounced, setDebounced] = useState(value);

  useEffect(() => {
    const id = setTimeout(() => setDebounced(value), delay);
    return () => clearTimeout(id);   // ← the cleanup is what makes it work
  }, [value, delay]);

  return debounced;
}

function Search() {
  const [query, setQuery] = useState('');
  const debouncedQuery = useDebounced(query, 300);

  useEffect(() => {
    if (debouncedQuery) fetchResults(debouncedQuery);
  }, [debouncedQuery]);

  return <input value={query} onChange={(e) => setQuery(e.target.value)} />;
}
```

Each keystroke cancels the pending timer via cleanup and starts a new one. The input stays responsive because it is not debounced — only the query is.

---

## Debouncing a handler

```jsx
import debounce from 'lodash.debounce';

function Editor({ save }) {
  // ❌ A new debounced function every render debounces nothing
  // const debouncedSave = debounce(save, 1000);

  const debouncedSave = useMemo(() => debounce(save, 1000), [save]);

  useEffect(() => () => debouncedSave.cancel(), [debouncedSave]);   // avoid firing after unmount

  return <textarea onChange={(e) => debouncedSave(e.target.value)} />;
}
```

Both details matter: without `useMemo` the timer resets every render, and without `cancel` you can call `setState` on an unmounted component.

---

## The React 18 alternative

```jsx
function Search() {
  const [query, setQuery] = useState('');
  const deferredQuery = useDeferredValue(query);

  return (
    <>
      <input value={query} onChange={(e) => setQuery(e.target.value)} />
      <Results query={deferredQuery} />   {/* renders at low priority */}
    </>
  );
}
```

No timers. React keeps the input responsive by rendering the expensive list at lower priority and interrupting it when the user types again.

**Use `useDeferredValue`** when the cost is *rendering*. **Use debounce** when the cost is a *network request* — deferring does not reduce how many requests you fire.

## Siblings have no direct channel

Data flows down. Two siblings have no reference to each other, so the shared value has to live above both.

```jsx
function Parent() {
  const [selectedId, setSelectedId] = useState(null);

  return (
    <>
      <List onSelect={setSelectedId} />        {/* writes */}
      <Detail id={selectedId} />                {/* reads */}
    </>
  );
}
```

---

## When they are far apart

**Context** — for values that are ambient and change rarely:

```jsx
const SelectionContext = createContext(null);

function Provider({ children }) {
  const [selectedId, setSelectedId] = useState(null);

  // Memoise, or every consumer re-renders whenever Provider re-renders
  const value = useMemo(() => ({ selectedId, setSelectedId }), [selectedId]);

  return <SelectionContext.Provider value={value}>{children}</SelectionContext.Provider>;
}
```

**A store** — for values that change often, because Context has no selector mechanism:

```jsx
import { create } from 'zustand';

const useStore = create((set) => ({
  selectedId: null,
  select: (id) => set({ selectedId: id }),
}));

// Each component subscribes to exactly the slice it reads
function List()   { const select = useStore((s) => s.select); … }
function Detail() { const id = useStore((s) => s.selectedId); … }
```

`List` never re-renders when `selectedId` changes, because it does not read it. Context cannot do that.

---

## Choosing

| Distance | Change frequency | Use |
|----------|------------------|-----|
| 1–2 levels | Any | Lift state up |
| Deep | Rare (theme, user) | Context |
| Deep | Frequent | Zustand / Jotai / Redux |
| Any | It is server data | React Query / SWR |

---

## The one people forget

If the shared state is **server data** — a user, a product list, search results — do not build any of this. React Query caches by key, so two distant components calling `useQuery(['user', id])` share one cache entry and one request automatically. No lifting, no context, no store.

A large share of "how do I share this state" questions are actually server-cache questions in disguise.
